 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of active cells, as each cell is processed independently without neighbor lookups or global connectivity checks [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the independent surface polygons, with no additional overhead for connectivity graphs or neighbor maps [S1].
-- **Measured Performance:** Enables interactive frame rates during dynamic LOD selection in resolution adaptive volume sculpting systems [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Avoids the computational cost of neighbor tracking and surface reconnection; maintains high display rates even in unconstrained hierarchies [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Visible cracks may appear between cells of different resolutions; not suitable for applications requiring watertight meshes or precise geometric continuity [S1].
-- **When to Use:** Interactive visualization and sculpting where frame rate is critical and minor visual artifacts are acceptable [S1].
-- **When Not to Use:** Applications requiring exportable, watertight meshes or high-fidelity static rendering where cracks are unacceptable [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Lower than reconnection-based methods because it avoids neighbor tracking and surface reconnection overhead [S1]. The cost is dominated by independent surface extraction per cell.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Lower memory overhead for mesh connectivity structures, as no global connectivity graph is maintained [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Applied in a resolution adaptive volume sculpting system to maintain interactive rates during dynamic LOD selection [S1]. Specific frame rates or hardware metrics are not provided in the sources.
+*   **Strengths:** High performance in dynamic environments; avoids the computational cost of neighbor tracking; suitable for unconstrained hierarchies where neighbors may be distant [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Visual cracks may appear, especially at low polygon densities; not suitable for applications requiring a watertight or connected mesh without post-processing; cracks are a fundamental issue of field resolution mismatches, not solvable by simply adding more triangles [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** Interactive sculpting or visualization where frame rate is critical and the surface is constantly changing [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Applications requiring a standard, connected mesh output without offline reconnection; static scenes where visual perfection is prioritized over speed [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the context of resolution adaptive volume sculpting by Eric Ferley, Marie-Paule Cani, and Jean-François Dufour in 2001 [S1]. It was designed to support interactive sculpting with a "virtual trackball" metaphor, allowing users to dynamically adjust resolution levels without being hindered by the computational cost of maintaining mesh connectivity [S1]. The approach contrasts with traditional multiresolution isosurfacing methods that prioritize geometric correctness over interactive performance [S1].
+The method was introduced in the context of "Resolution Adaptive Volume Sculpting" by Eric Ferley, Marie-Paule Cani, and Jean-François Dufour [S1]. It was developed to address the need for interactive performance in dynamic sculpting systems where users manipulate scalar fields in real-time [S1]. The approach contrasts with other multiresolution isosurface constructions that pay attention to cracks and enforce connectivity [S1]. The system was demonstrated with examples sculpted using a 2D-mouse with a "virtual trackball" metaphor, and additional material was available at the INRIA Imagis lab website [S1].
 
